<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ustertag</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ustertag was a mass assembly of some 10,000 people from the countryside of the canton of Zürich, held at Uster on 22 November 1830. Its Uster Memorial demanded a new constitution with representation according to population and equal rights for town and country. The resulting liberal constitution of 1831 began the era of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regeneration-switzerland">Regeneration</a> in Swiss cantonal politics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ustertag.jpg" alt="Ustertag" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hochgeladen von sidonius 07:27, 24 May 2006 (UTC) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bild_Tag_von_Uster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the city of Zürich acquired a number of rural areas, whose poor and uneducated population remained under its political and economic control. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a> of 1798, which was French controlled, the medieval idea of different laws for city citizens and country peasants was overthrown, but the republic collapsed in 1803 and was replaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-mediation">Act of Mediation</a>, which was itself overturned after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s defeat in 1813. The new constitution of the Restoration, which began in 1814, reduced the representation of the rural areas in the cantonal councils.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After the French July Revolution of 1830, revolutionary movements sprang up across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, with assemblies near Weinfelden in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgau">Thurgau</a> and then at Wohlenschwil in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a> and Sursee in Lucerne. In Zürich pamphlets such as the Memorial of Küsnacht argued that the canton should embrace the changes sweeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and about a hundred men meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafa">Stäfa</a> chose Uster, the most central place in the canton, for a gathering. The St. Andreas Church there could not hold the crowd, and the assembly moved to the nearby Zimiker hill. After a day of speeches the participants went home peaceably, without rioting or violence.</p><p>The assemblies of 1830–31 made two main demands of the cantonal constitutions: a new allocation of seats in the local legislatures and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagsatzung">Tagsatzung</a>, ending what they saw as the over-representation of the cantonal capital, and a way of amending the constitutions, which very few cantons had.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 6 December a new Cantonal Council was elected with two-thirds of its members from the rural areas, and by March 1831 a new constitution addressing most of the points of the Ustertag was in force. The reforms that followed the assemblies ended the Restoration and led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regeneration-switzerland">Regeneration</a>, the Constitution of 1848 and the Swiss federal state. The day of the Ustertag is still marked by keynote speeches in the Swiss Reformed Church.</p><p>The assemblies of 1830–31 were known collectively in Swiss German as Putsch, and the word passed into standard German as a political term, popularised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-keller">Gottfried Keller</a>. The name Ustertag joins the name of the town with Tag, from Tagung, a political assembly.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ustertag” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Napoleon’s Act of Mediation of 1803 restored Zürich as a canton of the Swiss Confederation, now with rural representation, and the liberal movement launched at the <b>Ustertag</b> of 1830 brought equal rights for town and country.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
